  "//gamma_rungs": "Rung 23 (sim_gamma, dense) RE-LANDED after the B1 varcorr sigma-hat^2 fix (2026-07-11 bug-fixes spec). Rung 24 (sim_sparse_gamma) RE-LANDED 2026-07-21: the backed-out failure was DIAGNOSED AND FIXED by the frontend RE-ordering change -- the parser used to extract slope REs before intercept REs, so the harness lowered (1|gp)+(1+x1..x4|ge) with ge as PRIMARY (q_p=5) and routed the DENSE kernel, whose joint optimizer lands ~2e-3 deviance above the tolPwrss=1e-13 lme4 optimum (beta[0] 1.9e-2 off). The frontend now lowers random effects in FORMULA ORDER (nested terms still sort first -- NestedWithin is relative to the primary), so gp is primary, the model routes SPARSE, and it lands 2e-4 above lme4 -- matching the in-crate golden that hand-builds the same orientation. Tolerances were NOT relaxed. STILL OPEN (recorded, not a rung gap): the dense joint optimizer's ~2e-3 shortfall on a q_p=5 primary with an extra grouping is real and reachable by writing the slope term first; the ordering fix stops ROUTING rung 24 there, it does not improve that path (dense stage-1/2 schedule work is a separate item). These are rung-18-style 2-WAY gates (no jl_formula): MixedModels' Gamma GLMM is a documented different estimator, its JSONs stay intentionally absent. See validation/README.md. GAMMA-INVERSE WITH RANDOM EFFECTS, SPARSE: attempted 2026-07-21 as spec sim_sparse_gamma_inv over the sim_sparse_gamma data and ABANDONED -- no oracle is obtainable there. That data is simulated under a LOG link and spans y 0.0045..265, so an inverse-link model is misspecified for it: eta goes negative, mu = 1/eta with it, and the Gamma likelihood is undefined. lme4 dies with 'PIRLS loop resulted in NaN value', and so does a plain fixed-only stats::glm on the same formula -- the failure is the data, not the mixed solver. Oracling this cell needs a dataset simulated under the inverse link, which is a new simulated fixture and a separate decision. The DENSE Gamma-inverse mixed cell is covered: sim_gamma_inv_glmm fits cleanly on sim_gamma.",
  "//m3_goldens": "M3 family/link/AGQ reference fits for the IN-CRATE frozen goldens (NOT curated rungs). Generated by engines/goldens_agq.R into validation/goldens/<name>.json -- a SEPARATE dir so compare.R's results/<engine>/*.json glob (the curated cross-engine sweep) is untouched, per design 6 'the curated 6-rung oracle is NOT expanded'. lme4/MASS only; no Julia/Rust cross-check. Every golden in goldens/ must have an entry here and therefore a generator -- sleepstudy_lmm, penicillin_lmm and pastes_lmm sat in goldens/ unregistered and unregenerable until 2026-07-21; they are the SAME datasets as rungs 2/3/4 but distinct model specs (pastes_lmm is the nested (1|batch/cask) form, rung 4 is the flat idiom), which is why they are separate goldens rather than rung results. Their files previously predated this script and carried its predecessor's schema (reml instead of nagq, 12-sig-fig rounding, and pastes_lmm's varcomp blocks in the opposite order to the cask:batch-first order lme4 emits today). Regenerating them reproduced every estimate (penicillin stddev to 6e-7, the rounding; pastes to 1e-7 once the two blocks are matched by name), so the regenerated form was ADOPTED 2026-07-21 and all three are now byte-reproducible from this script. Adopting it required first converting every multi-block varcomp read in the crate to key by group NAME rather than index: glmm emits declaration order, lme4's VarCorr emits descending level count, and pastes_lmm is the fixture where the two disagree.",
